1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,270
Ground Truth SQL.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:04,830
I'm Riya Kapadnis, and this is my final project

3
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:05,570
film.

4
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:09,210
The problem: AI can write database code that

5
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:12,350
runs with no errors, but gives the wrong answer.

6
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:14,487
Picture an analyst asking an AI:

7
00:00:15,027 --> 00:00:16,467
what is our revenue by country?

8
00:00:16,927 --> 00:00:20,247
The AI writes SQL, the language for asking a

9
00:00:20,247 --> 00:00:21,487
database questions.

10
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:22,527
It runs.

11
00:00:22,827 --> 00:00:23,767
No errors.

12
00:00:24,227 --> 00:00:26,597
It says the USA made $4,667.

13
00:00:28,227 --> 00:00:30,747
The real answer is $523.

14
00:00:30,992 --> 00:00:31,992
Why does this matter?

15
00:00:32,332 --> 00:00:34,992
People ask data questions in plain English,

16
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:36,652
so most never see the SQL.

17
00:00:37,412 --> 00:00:39,292
The AI doesn't know your data.

18
00:00:39,492 --> 00:00:41,512
And wrong SQL doesn't crash.

19
00:00:41,952 --> 00:00:43,832
It just gives a number people trust,

20
00:00:43,932 --> 00:00:45,872
and that number ends up in reports and

21
00:00:45,872 --> 00:00:46,432
decisions.

22
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:48,940
So I'm building Ground Truth SQL:

23
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:51,635
a test for AI data assistants.

24
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:53,900
Give it your own data and questions,

25
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:56,860
and it tells you which answers the AI got wrong,

26
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:57,840
and why.

27
00:00:57,867 --> 00:00:58,967
Where will it be used?

28
00:00:59,367 --> 00:01:01,427
Anywhere people ask data questions in plain

29
00:01:01,427 --> 00:01:04,107
English: store sales, finance,

30
00:01:04,187 --> 00:01:07,392
hospitals, marketing, and AI data assistants.

31
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:09,907
Analysts learn what to double check.

32
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:12,287
Teams test a tool before buying.

33
00:01:13,007 --> 00:01:14,327
Engineers see what to fix.

34
00:01:14,408 --> 00:01:16,568
Others have tested AI on SQL.

35
00:01:17,528 --> 00:01:19,008
BIRD is a public benchmark:

36
00:01:19,143 --> 00:01:23,388
12,751 questions over 95 databases,

37
00:01:23,548 --> 00:01:25,328
scored by checking results.

38
00:01:25,928 --> 00:01:29,628
People score about 93%, and the best AI still

39
00:01:29,628 --> 00:01:30,688
scores lower.

40
00:01:31,188 --> 00:01:34,008
But BIRD tests AI only on its own databases,

41
00:01:34,508 --> 00:01:35,128
not yours.

42
00:01:35,325 --> 00:01:38,005
Tools like Vanna write SQL on your own data,

43
00:01:38,005 --> 00:01:40,745
and come with a built-in answer test.

44
00:01:41,005 --> 00:01:42,885
But that test looks for words,

45
00:01:43,065 --> 00:01:44,125
not right numbers.

46
00:01:44,665 --> 00:01:47,025
On my data, it passed the answer that was nine

47
00:01:47,025 --> 00:01:48,445
times too high.

48
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:49,450
So there's a gap.

49
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:52,590
Benchmarks check numbers, but not on your data.

50
00:01:53,190 --> 00:01:55,130
Tools work on your data, but don't check the

51
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:55,710
numbers.

52
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,270
Ground Truth SQL does both,

53
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:00,410
so you find the wrong answers before anyone

54
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:01,408
trusts them.

55
00:02:01,408 --> 00:02:04,368
The data: Chinook, a free sample music store

56
00:02:04,368 --> 00:02:05,168
database.

57
00:02:05,648 --> 00:02:10,128
11 tables, 59 customers, and 412 orders from

58
00:02:10,128 --> 00:02:12,148
2009 to 2013.

59
00:02:12,283 --> 00:02:14,643
Step one: write about 50 questions,

60
00:02:14,683 --> 00:02:16,223
each with a checked answer.

61
00:02:16,783 --> 00:02:18,743
I check every answer a second way,

62
00:02:18,823 --> 00:02:21,563
in plain Python, straight from the raw tables.

63
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:24,300
Step two: Claude answers each question three

64
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:25,060
times.

65
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,050
It sees only the table layout and the question,

66
00:02:28,050 --> 00:02:30,420
and may run one read-only query.

67
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,640
It can never change the data.

68
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:34,860
Step three: judge by results,

69
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,180
not wording, since one answer can be written

70
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:38,140
many ways.

71
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:40,320
These two queries look different,

72
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,400
but both give 523.

73
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:45,540
Step four: a report card that sorts every miss

74
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:48,940
by its cause: counting an order once per song,

75
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:50,880
mixing up milliseconds and seconds,

76
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,300
counting purchases instead of people,

77
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:55,040
or linking the wrong columns.

78
00:02:55,242 --> 00:02:57,582
What I need: a laptop, Python,

79
00:02:57,722 --> 00:02:58,762
and the free database.

80
00:02:59,462 --> 00:03:02,152
No cloud servers, and no extra token credits:

81
00:03:02,152 --> 00:03:04,722
Claude answers through my Claude Code plan,

82
00:03:04,722 --> 00:03:07,422
the same way it did in my first runs.

83
00:03:07,617 --> 00:03:09,617
The finished project will have one sample

84
00:03:09,617 --> 00:03:12,137
database, about 50 test questions,

85
00:03:12,137 --> 00:03:14,777
and Claude as the AI it tests.

86
00:03:15,337 --> 00:03:17,877
It won't be a chat app, and it never changes the

87
00:03:17,877 --> 00:03:18,337
data.

88
00:03:18,408 --> 00:03:20,368
First, does the checker judge correctly?

89
00:03:20,948 --> 00:03:23,648
On 9 test queries that I labeled by hand,

90
00:03:23,788 --> 00:03:26,048
comparing wording judged 6 right.

91
00:03:26,548 --> 00:03:28,508
Comparing results: all 9.

92
00:03:28,616 --> 00:03:31,036
Then I had Claude answer my 8 questions,

93
00:03:31,156 --> 00:03:32,056
three times.

94
00:03:32,596 --> 00:03:34,676
The checker passed 22 of 24.

95
00:03:35,316 --> 00:03:37,516
The two fails were a tie my question never

96
00:03:37,516 --> 00:03:38,176
settled.

97
00:03:38,496 --> 00:03:41,056
Claude wasn't wrong; my question was.

98
00:03:41,116 --> 00:03:43,476
It also caught a mistake in my answer key.

99
00:03:44,056 --> 00:03:46,336
One question asked about 2023,

100
00:03:46,576 --> 00:03:48,556
but the data ends in 2013.

101
00:03:49,296 --> 00:03:50,936
The right answer was empty,

102
00:03:51,096 --> 00:03:54,316
so broken SQL returning nothing looked correct.

103
00:03:54,936 --> 00:03:57,156
Now the tool rejects empty answers.

104
00:03:57,366 --> 00:03:59,426
It worked if the checker agrees with my hand

105
00:03:59,426 --> 00:04:02,786
labels every time, every answer key passes the

106
00:04:02,786 --> 00:04:05,466
second check, and every miss on the report card

107
00:04:05,466 --> 00:04:06,706
has a named cause.

108
00:04:07,286 --> 00:04:09,326
It didn't work if Claude gets everything right,

109
00:04:09,326 --> 00:04:12,246
because that means my questions are too easy.

110
00:04:12,666 --> 00:04:15,246
Today it nearly does, so the next 50 questions

111
00:04:15,246 --> 00:04:16,326
must be harder.

112
00:04:16,408 --> 00:04:19,248
In conclusion: AI can give wrong answers that

113
00:04:19,248 --> 00:04:19,988
look right.

114
00:04:20,708 --> 00:04:23,408
Ground Truth SQL tests the AI on your own data

115
00:04:23,408 --> 00:04:25,468
and shows what's wrong, and why.

116
00:04:26,108 --> 00:04:27,128
Today the checker works.

117
00:04:27,748 --> 00:04:29,508
Next: 50 harder questions.

118
00:04:29,988 --> 00:04:33,108
So the takeaway is this: running SQL is easy.

119
00:04:33,668 --> 00:04:34,748
Being right is the test.
